1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,932
Lesson 9.2.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:03,599
Discrete diffusion.

3
00:00:04,717 --> 00:00:08,112
Text is made of symbols,
not real numbers, so

4
00:00:08,112 --> 00:00:11,206
Gaussian diffusion does not fit directly.

5
00:00:11,586 --> 00:00:15,930
Discrete diffusion replaces Gaussian
noise with symbol corruption.

6
00:00:16,310 --> 00:00:19,980
The most successful version is
masked diffusion: tokens are

7
00:00:19,980 --> 00:00:23,339
gradually hidden, and the
model learns to reveal them.

8
00:00:23,719 --> 00:00:27,332
It is the basis of diffusion
language models and, as we will

9
00:00:27,332 --> 00:00:30,584
see, it fits hardware that
samples discrete variables.

10
00:00:31,717 --> 00:00:35,715
At time t between zero and
one, each token is replaced by

11
00:00:35,715 --> 00:00:39,083
a mask symbol with
probability t, independently.

12
00:00:39,463 --> 00:00:44,367
The mask is an absorbing state:
once hidden, the token stays hidden.

13
00:00:44,747 --> 00:00:48,138
At t = 1 the sequence is fully masked.

14
00:00:48,518 --> 00:00:52,543
The reverse process starts from all
masks and reveals tokens, using a

15
00:00:52,543 --> 00:00:56,044
model that predicts each hidden
token given what is visible.

16
00:00:57,150 --> 00:01:02,081
The masked diffusion language model,
MDLM, trains with a lower bound

17
00:01:02,081 --> 00:01:06,360
on the likelihood that turns
out to be surprisingly simple:

18
00:01:06,740 --> 00:01:11,198
a cross-entropy over the
masked positions, weighted by a

19
00:01:11,198 --> 00:01:16,373
time-dependent factor, which for
a linear schedule is one over t.

20
00:01:16,753 --> 00:01:20,885
It is like BERT training, but
with all masking rates and with

21
00:01:20,885 --> 00:01:24,408
a weight that turns the sum
into a likelihood bound.

22
00:01:25,517 --> 00:01:30,367
When sampling, revealing one token per
step is exact if the model is perfect.

23
00:01:30,747 --> 00:01:33,366
But it costs as many steps as tokens.

24
00:01:33,746 --> 00:01:36,978
Revealing several at once
is faster, but samples

25
00:01:36,978 --> 00:01:40,211
them independently,
ignoring their correlations.

26
00:01:40,591 --> 00:01:43,968
We measure it with highly
correlated binary strings of

27
00:01:43,968 --> 00:01:47,095
eight symbols, where almost
half the sequences are

28
00:01:47,095 --> 00:01:50,598
constant, and a model that
knows the exact conditionals.

29
00:01:51,717 --> 00:01:56,575
With eight steps, one per token,
the total variation distance to

30
00:01:56,575 --> 00:02:00,750
the true distribution is only
0.03, the sampling noise.

31
00:02:01,130 --> 00:02:03,193
With four steps it rises to 0.2.

32
00:02:03,573 --> 00:02:05,667
With two, to 0.46.

33
00:02:06,047 --> 00:02:09,817
And with a single step,
revealing everything at once, to

34
00:02:09,817 --> 00:02:13,723
0.79: constant sequences, which
are forty-eight percent of

35
00:02:13,723 --> 00:02:17,157
the data, appear less than
one percent of the time.

36
00:02:17,537 --> 00:02:20,948
The error does not come from the
model, but from parallel sampling.

37
00:02:22,050 --> 00:02:26,725
That is why few-step samplers need
tricks: choosing which tokens to reveal

38
00:02:26,725 --> 00:02:31,336
based on confidence, or distilling a
many-step model into a few-step one.

39
00:02:31,716 --> 00:02:36,062
Probabilistic hardware offers another
way out: instead of sampling a

40
00:02:36,062 --> 00:02:40,344
step's tokens independently, it
can sample their joint distribution

41
00:02:40,344 --> 00:02:44,178
with an energy-based model that
captures their correlations.

42
00:02:44,558 --> 00:02:48,303
That is the idea of denoising
thermodynamic models.

43
00:02:49,417 --> 00:02:53,746
Discrete diffusion is used in
non-autoregressive language models, which

44
00:02:53,746 --> 00:02:58,197
generate in parallel and allow infilling
or editing any part of the text.

45
00:02:58,577 --> 00:03:01,919
In lab thirteen you will
train a small MDLM and

46
00:03:01,919 --> 00:03:05,049
measure quality against
the number of steps.

47
00:03:06,150 --> 00:03:09,655
Common mistakes: forgetting
that the mask is absorbing.

48
00:03:10,035 --> 00:03:13,773
Removing the time weight, so
the loss is no longer a bound.

49
00:03:14,153 --> 00:03:18,029
Revealing many correlated tokens
at once and blaming the model.

50
00:03:18,409 --> 00:03:22,006
And comparing step counts without
matching model evaluations.

51
00:03:23,117 --> 00:03:27,092
In summary: masked diffusion
hides tokens forward and

52
00:03:27,092 --> 00:03:31,443
reveals them backward, with a
weighted cross-entropy loss.

53
00:03:31,823 --> 00:03:36,175
Revealing one token per step
is exact; revealing many at

54
00:03:36,175 --> 00:03:39,595
once samples them
independently and destroys

55
00:03:39,595 --> 00:03:44,181
correlations, in our example
from 0.03 to 0.79 in distance.

56
00:03:44,561 --> 00:03:48,681
Sampling the joint is exactly what
thermodynamic hardware can contribute.

57
00:03:49,783 --> 00:03:53,518
To practice: give the
probability of masking at time t.

58
00:03:53,898 --> 00:03:57,043
Explain why revealing
one per step is exact.

59
00:03:57,423 --> 00:04:01,739
Analyze two perfectly correlated
bits with a one-step sampler.

60
00:04:02,119 --> 00:04:04,515
And name two ways to reduce the steps.
